stream service is rate-limited, injecting repeated retry iterations directly into the saturated dependency multiplies request density. This creates a self-reinforcing queue inflation loop: blocked workers retain in-flight tasks while executing backoff delays, causing task execution duration to surge from 1.8 to 14.2 seconds (E03). Crucially, this mechanism explains why horizontal scaling failed: increasing active worker instances from 8 to 16 at 09:20 yielded a negligible 6% queue depth reduction over a ten-minute observation window (E07). Adding parallel worker concurrency without addressing dependency capacity merely intensified outbound request pressure against dependency B, provoking sustained HTTP 429 throttling and consuming worker threads in idle backoff cycles. The invalidity of worker starvation as a primary cause was further corroborated at 09:26: when retries toward dependency B were temporarily disabled, queue depth plummeted 38% in six minutes despite an elevated direct error rate (E08), proving that retry looping, rather than compute insufficiency, maintained queue congestion.\n\n3. Critique of Existing Retry Policy\nTelemetry reveals critical structural deficiencies in the existing retry architecture. First, the 420% explosion in retry traffic upon receiving HTTP 429 status codes demonstrates the absence of request shedding or adaptive rate matching (E06). Retrying immediately against an already saturated upstream dependency violates rate-limit contracts and induces classic retry storms. Second, while exponential backoff was present, it lacked coordination mechanisms such as full random jitter, concurrency throttling, or rate-aware circuit breaking. When 31% of interactions failed (E05), each failed task consumed up to three sequential backoff cycles, locking worker capacity and ballooning task latency nearly eightfold (E03). Third, the policy lacked differentiation between transient network anomalies and upstream quota enforcement; treating HTTP 429 