on jitter, and enforcing hard per-task deadline timeouts to prevent unbounded worker thread blocking.\nPhase 2 (Protective Circuit Breaking): Implement an adaptive, client-side circuit breaker pattern around dependency B that monitors HTTP 429 error ratios. If the failure threshold exceeds 10% within a rolling 30-second window, the circuit must open, immediately failing fast or diverting requests to an asynchronous dead-letter buffer rather than stalling workers in backoff loops.\nPhase 3 (Rate-Limiting Alignment and Worker Autoscaling Constraints): Introduce token-bucket or leaky-bucket egress rate limiters on outbound calls to dependency B configured strictly within negotiated contractual quotas. Furthermore, decouple worker scaling policies from raw queue depth metrics: worker autoscaling must be gated by external dependency health indicators to avoid deploying additional workers that worsen throttle states.\nPhase 4 (Observability and Contractual Negotiation): Establish granular telemetry tracking outbound quota consumption, per-dependency latency percentiles, and retry amplification ratios. Concurrently, formalize service-level agreements and dynamic burst allocations with the operators of dependency B to ensure provider capacity accommodates anticipated peak workloads.",
